From 317d9f303b63f9e7d86cdd575139392738c0250f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Golle <daniel@makrotopia.org>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 07:27:22 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] pipeline: rkisp2: Drive the ISPP on the RV1126

On the RV1126 the ISP main path can feed the ISPP post-processor
through an in-kernel pool, with the VICAP feeding the ISP inside the
kernel as well; the ISPP main output then carries the processed frames
and the ISPP params node takes the module parameters. Match the ISPP
media device when it exists and run the pipe through it:

 - the readback link stays disabled, the ISP input takes the sensor
   format and the ISPP input pad follows the ISPP output pad of the ISP,
   which decides the pipe size;
 - the ISPP main output is the capture node of the stream, at the pipe
   size and in the formats the node offers;
 - the ISPP params buffers circulate through the IPA. The root start of
   the ISPP waits for the params stream and its first buffer, whose
   blocks set the module mask, so the start fills every buffer with the
   synchronous IPA call and queues it before the params node streams
   on: the asynchronous reply of the per-frame call could only be
   dispatched after the blocking start of the main output returned;
 - the ISP params and statistics nodes run as before for the 3A;
 - the delayed sensor controls are pushed and applied from the ISP
   frame start event, one slot per frame, with the controls of the
   latest request. The ISP runs free, so requests are not aligned with
   the frames: a push per request would repeat or skip slots, which
   DelayedControls reports as warnings. With the pool pipe the ISP
   starts a frame while the sensor already exposes the second frame
   after it: a control written at the ISP frame start of frame S is
   first visible in frame S+3 on the RV1126, measured with
   sequence-tagged frames, so the hook applies the controls for that
   lag plus the sensor delays.

Without an ISPP media device the ISP main path remains the output.

Signed-off-by: Daniel Golle <daniel@makrotopia.org>
